From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Medium1992 <Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 00:06:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat(sniff): add a STUN sniffer

A routing rule protocol ["stun"] now matches STUN (RFC 8489, RFC 5389)
and the TURN messages that share its header (RFC 8656), over UDP and TCP:
the ICE checks of WebRTC and of WhatsApp, Telegram and FaceTime calls,
and their relay allocations. ICE sends the media after its checks on the
same ports, so the rule takes the whole call: to a nearby node, direct,
or blocked. There is no domain to override.

A message must carry the magic cookie 0x2112A442, have its first two bits
zero, a length that is a multiple of 4, and attributes that end where the
message does. Over UDP it is the whole first datagram: the dispatcher now
hands a sniffer the flow's datagrams apart where it asks for them. Over
TCP the sniffer waits for the rest once the cookie is in. Classic STUN
(RFC 3489) has no cookie, and without one a header is a few bits any
datagram may carry, so it is left out. The header excludes what the
other UDP sniffers take: QUIC long headers set the first bit, uTP starts
with 0x41 and the DHT with 'd', and neither DNS nor a UDP tracker has the
cookie there.

Tested with the sample request of RFC 5769, checked by its FINGERPRINT,
against DNS, uTP, DHT, UDP tracker, the QUIC and TLS corpora, and by
fuzzing; and end to end on a real Xray process, where the rule blocks
STUN over UDP and TCP and DNS, plain UDP and TCP pass. The same test
against the v26.10.31-h2Flow-1 release fails for every STUN case.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
